WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.428
(mysterious music)

00:00:03.428 --> 00:00:04.968
- [Voiceover] C-sections are
the most common major surgery

00:00:04.968 --> 00:00:07.135
performed on human beings.

00:00:08.688 --> 00:00:10.998
Over basically one generation of moms,

00:00:10.998 --> 00:00:14.269
the C-section rate has gone up by 500%.

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:16.848
The problem is that
your number one risk for

00:00:16.848 --> 00:00:19.859
having a C-section might be
which hospital you go to.

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:21.749
That's what wakes me up in the morning.

00:00:21.749 --> 00:00:24.666
(mysterious music)

00:00:28.788 --> 00:00:31.219
I'm Neel Shah, an obstetrician over at

00:00:31.219 --> 00:00:33.818
Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center.

00:00:33.818 --> 00:00:35.979
I'm an assistant professor
at Harvard Medical School,

00:00:35.979 --> 00:00:39.688
and I'm part of the core
faculty here at Ariadne Labs.

00:00:39.688 --> 00:00:42.248
A lot of people don't
realize the full magnitude

00:00:42.248 --> 00:00:44.831
of the problem with C-sections.

00:00:46.448 --> 00:00:49.880
Basically, in the late 1960s, early 1970s,

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.008
C-section rates were about five percent.

00:00:52.008 --> 00:00:53.439
Then all of a sudden, the C-section rate

00:00:53.439 --> 00:00:54.829
starts to skyrocket.

00:00:54.829 --> 00:00:56.566
Not just increase, but skyrocket.

00:00:56.566 --> 00:00:58.008
So, before the end of the century,

00:00:58.008 --> 00:00:59.966
the C-section rate goes
up to one in three.

00:00:59.966 --> 00:01:04.048
And we don't really have a great
idea why this is happening.

00:01:04.048 --> 00:01:06.099
As a surgeon myself, it's hard to believe

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:07.528
that one in three human beings needs

00:01:07.528 --> 00:01:09.080
major surgery to be born.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.578
But that's sort of where we are right now.

00:01:10.578 --> 00:01:12.687
And the consequences are significant.

00:01:12.687 --> 00:01:15.520
So, things like severe
infection, hemorrhage,

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.288
organ injury, in addition to
five billion dollars of spending annually,

00:01:20.288 --> 00:01:23.008
we're talking something like 20,000 major,

00:01:23.008 --> 00:01:25.888
avoidable surgical
complications that we're seeing

00:01:25.888 --> 00:01:29.406
from C-sections we didn't
need to do in the first place.

00:01:29.406 --> 00:01:31.949
I was really puzzled when
I first started this job,

00:01:31.949 --> 00:01:34.339
thinking like, what can
we possibly do about this.

00:01:34.339 --> 00:01:36.947
Epidemiologists,
economists, policy makers,

00:01:36.947 --> 00:01:38.309
lots of people have
been thinking about this

00:01:38.309 --> 00:01:39.438
for a long time.

00:01:39.438 --> 00:01:42.240
The clue for me was the
fact that across country,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.579
C-section rates vary from seven
percent to 70% by hospital.

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:49.080
If hospital performance is so uneven,

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.909
and it's not explained by patient
risk or patient preference,

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:54.807
that means the hospital
itself could be a risk factor.

00:01:54.807 --> 00:01:56.740
All of our projects right
now are actually trying to

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.694
figure out what makes hospital
A different from hospital B.

00:01:59.694 --> 00:02:02.611
(electronic music)

00:02:09.713 --> 00:02:11.686
What we've seen is that often times the

00:02:11.686 --> 00:02:13.262
labor floor is in kind of like an

00:02:13.262 --> 00:02:14.934
almost forgotten about corner and

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:16.891
it just doesn't get a lot of investment

00:02:16.891 --> 00:02:18.715
or attention from the hospital.

00:02:18.715 --> 00:02:21.115
When we visit a bunch
of different hospitals,

00:02:21.115 --> 00:02:23.355
we realized that they're
laid out very differently,

00:02:23.355 --> 00:02:24.595
and that might matter.

00:02:24.595 --> 00:02:26.806
For example, there are
no rules for how many

00:02:26.806 --> 00:02:28.704
labor floor rooms you should have

00:02:28.704 --> 00:02:31.542
based on the amount of
patients that you see.

00:02:31.542 --> 00:02:34.315
The place that does
twice as many deliveries

00:02:34.315 --> 00:02:36.134
also does way more C-sections.

00:02:36.134 --> 00:02:37.606
The only way that's possible is if they're

00:02:37.606 --> 00:02:39.494
moving people through much faster.

00:02:39.494 --> 00:02:41.635
Almost all labor floors seem really

00:02:41.635 --> 00:02:44.464
tight on resources, so
the idea is when you have

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.753
a huge surgeon patient volume, how do you

00:02:46.753 --> 00:02:48.685
flexibly recruit more rooms?

00:02:48.685 --> 00:02:50.566
How do you bring in more staff?

00:02:50.566 --> 00:02:53.444
You report C-section rates
back to your doctors.

00:02:53.444 --> 00:02:55.275
Like, I have no idea what my C-section rate is

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:56.794
until somebody tells me.

00:02:56.794 --> 00:02:59.006
Now we're testing this hypothesis
that management matters

00:02:59.006 --> 00:03:00.486
in childbirth just the way it matters

00:03:00.486 --> 00:03:02.486
in every other industry.

00:03:03.355 --> 00:03:06.354
We're starting to parse
differences among C-sections.

00:03:06.354 --> 00:03:09.413
So, rather than looking at
all C-sections all together,

00:03:09.413 --> 00:03:11.755
we're trying to just take
the lowest risk women

00:03:11.755 --> 00:03:15.387
and focus on their C-section rate.

00:03:15.387 --> 00:03:18.126
As soon as you start to do
that, two things happen: 

00:03:18.126 --> 00:03:20.104
Insurance companies
start to pay attention,

00:03:20.104 --> 00:03:22.646
and then, just women
start to pay attention.

00:03:22.646 --> 00:03:24.184
And I actually think this is an area where

00:03:24.184 --> 00:03:26.537
women's views on this are probably

00:03:26.537 --> 00:03:30.915
going to be the driving
force behind the change.

00:03:30.915 --> 00:03:32.955
There's really no other
moment in people's life

00:03:32.955 --> 00:03:34.064
where they're so discerning

00:03:34.064 --> 00:03:36.564
as when they become pregnant.

